##1.2 Trend Analysis

Velocity decreased from 32 points in Sprint 1 to 29 points in Sprint 2, a drop of 3 points (about 9.4%). The team's average velocity through Sprint 2 is 30.5 points per sprint. The decline is small and does not indicate a performance problem, because both sprints finished 100% of their committed scope with no carryover.

Three factors likely explain the difference:

- **Change in work type:** Sprint 1 was entirely backend and integration work (`epic:doms`). Sprint 2 moved to customer-facing BOPIS features (`epic:bopis`) plus the start of the mobile app (`epic:mobile-app`), which required coordination across checkout, associate, and customer notification flows.
- **Story size:** Sprint 2 included two 8-point items (#11 Automate Order Allocation and #15 Real-Time Inventory Audit), which concentrated effort and review time into a few large tasks.
- **Dependencies:** The notification and hand-off items (#12, #13, #14) depended on order allocation and store pickup selection (#11, #10) being finished first.

Because the team met its commitment in both sprints, velocity is stable and predictable. This supports the Sprint 3 commitment of 28 points, which is below the 30.5-point average and therefore achievable.

## 1.3 Burn-Up Chart



Completed story points against total project scope through the end of Sprint 2

What the chart shows:

- Completed work reached 61 of 89 total story points (68.5%) by the end of Sprint 2.
- Planned progress at the end of Sprint 2 is 2/3 of total scope (about 59 points), so the team finished roughly 2 points ahead of plan.
- Completed work rose in a stepwise pattern in each sprint with no carryover, and the 28 points remaining match the Sprint 3 commitment.
- Total scope stayed flat at 89 points. The deferred curbside geo-arrival items were excluded per the CCB decision, so scope creep did not affect the burn-up.
- At the current average velocity of 30.5 points per sprint, the remaining 28 points can be completed within Sprint 3 (Nov 09 – Nov 22), well ahead of the December 4, 2026 deadline.
